---
import type { ExperienceRole } from "../types/emdash";
import CareerEntry from "./CareerEntry.astro";

interface Props {
  roles: ExperienceRole[];
}

const { roles } = Astro.props;
---

<section id="career" class="scroll-mt-28">
  <header class="max-w-2xl">
    <p class="act-kicker">Act one — Trust</p>
    <h2 class="act-title">The relationship was always the infrastructure.</h2>

    <div class="mt-7 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-400">
      <p>
        I started at Global Resource Group as one of its first three employees, selling GPS
        telematics to people who had never heard of it. No brand, no case studies, no obvious
        reason for anyone to pick up the phone. So the work became the thing it has been ever
        since: get curious fast, understand what someone is actually trying to fix, and be the
        person who follows through.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is how a $1M book got built one relationship at a time, how a 500-device nationwide
        rollout held together, and how I ended up running operations for three companies and a
        hundred accounts. The systems changed. The job never did.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="path-only mt-8" data-path-for="hiring">
      <p class="path-note">
        Fifteen years, four companies, one referral engine: a 90% close rate on relationships I
        earned, 130% quota three years running, and an invitation onto a client&apos;s podcast
        because the work was worth talking about.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <aside class="path-only mt-8" data-path-for="build">
      <p class="path-note">
        Every system I have built since started here: sitting with the person who has the
        problem long enough to know what actually hurts.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </header>

  <div class="mt-14 space-y-16">
    {roles.map((role) => <CareerEntry role={role} />)}
  </div>
</section>
